    <!-- ============ TL;DR (ANSWER-FIRST) ============ -->
    <section class="tldr-section">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="tldr-box">
          <h2>What is Agricultural Water Management in India?</h2>
          <p>Agricultural water management in India is the strategic practice of optimizing water use to ensure food security amid severe groundwater depletion. The core solution is transitioning from flood irrigation to <strong>micro-irrigation (drip/sprinkler)</strong>, which achieves <strong>80–95% efficiency</strong>, supported by <strong>PMKSY subsidies</strong> (55% for small farmers) and practices like <strong>Direct Seeded Rice</strong> and <strong>laser land leveling</strong>.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ THREE PILLARS ============ -->
    <section class="pillars" id="technologies">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>The Three Pillars of India’s Water Management Strategy</h2>
        <div class="pillar-grid">
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">💧</div>
            <h3>Technology &amp; On-Farm Efficiency (The “Hardware”)</h3>
            <p><strong>Micro-Irrigation (Drip &amp; Sprinkler):</strong> Delivers water directly to the root zone, minimizing evaporation and runoff. Drip systems are the gold standard for water-scarce regions, achieving up to 90–95% efficiency.</p>
            <p><strong>Precision Scheduling:</strong> Moving beyond fixed timers. Using <strong>soil moisture sensors</strong> and <strong>evapotranspiration (ET) models</strong> to irrigate only when the crop needs it, saving up to <strong>30–45%</strong> water compared to traditional methods.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">🏛️</div>
            <h3>Policy &amp; Economic Incentives (The “Enabler”)</h3>
            <p><strong>PMKSY &amp; PDMC:</strong> The umbrella scheme (“Har Khet Ko Pani”) provides the financial backbone. The <strong>“Per Drop More Crop” (PDMC)</strong> component offers <strong>55% subsidy</strong> for small/marginal farmers and <strong>45%</strong> for others to install micro-irrigation systems.</p>
            <p><strong>Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF):</strong> Managed by NABARD, this provides additional state-level subsidies, making the upfront capital cost less prohibitive for smallholders.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pillar-card">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">🌱</div>
            <h3>Agronomy &amp; Landscape Practices (The “Method”)</h3>
            <p><strong>Crop Diversification:</strong> Shifting away from water-intensive paddy in the Northwest (Punjab/Haryana) towards pulses, oilseeds, and millets. This is critical as Haryana’s groundwater extraction is at <strong>136.75%</strong>.</p>
            <p><strong>Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) &amp; AWD:</strong> DSR eliminates the need for continuous flooding during transplantation. <strong>Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD)</strong> can reduce water use by up to 30% without yield loss.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ COMPARISON TABLE ============ -->
    <section class="comparison" id="compare">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>Comparison of Irrigation Methods: Which is Right for Your Farm?</h2>
        <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" aria-label="Irrigation Methods Comparison Table" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Feature / Method</th>
                <th scope="col">Drip Irrigation</th>
                <th scope="col">Sprinkler Irrigation</th>
                <th scope="col">Flood / Furrow Irrigation</th>
                <th scope="col">Rainwater Harvesting (Farm Ponds)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Water Use Efficiency</th>
                <td><strong>80–95%</strong></td>
                <td>70–85%</td>
                <td>30–50%</td>
                <td>Variable (storage dependent)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Initial Cost</th>
                <td>High (subsidized up to 55%)</td>
                <td>Medium</td>
                <td>Low</td>
                <td>Medium–High (earthwork)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Best For</th>
                <td>Orchards, vegetables, wide-row crops (cotton, sugarcane)</td>
                <td>Cereals, pulses, uneven terrain</td>
                <td>Rice, flat land with abundant water</td>
                <td>Drought-proofing, supplemental irrigation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Fertigation Support</th>
                <td><strong>Excellent</strong> (direct nutrient delivery)</td>
                <td>Good</td>
                <td>Poor</td>
                <td>Not applicable</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Key Drawback</th>
                <td>High clogging risk if water is unfiltered</td>
                <td>High evaporation loss in windy conditions</td>
                <td>Severe water wastage and waterlogging</td>
                <td>Requires significant land area and rainfall</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Subsidy Available (PDMC)</th>
                <td>Yes (55% for small farmers)</td>
                <td>Yes (55% for small farmers)</td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>Yes (via MGNREGA / State schemes)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaways">
          <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>For maximum water savings and high-value crops,</strong> Drip irrigation is the clear winner, despite higher upfront costs that are heavily subsidized.</li>
            <li><strong>For field crops on uneven terrain,</strong> Sprinklers offer a pragmatic middle ground.</li>
            <li><strong>Flood irrigation is the baseline “villain”</strong> of India’s water crisis and should be phased out where possible.</li>
            <li><strong>Farm ponds are a complementary strategy,</strong> not a replacement for efficient application methods, especially in Maharashtra’s drought-prone zones.</li>
          </ul>
