            <p class="stat-desc">Available per beneficiary family, with a <strong>7-year repeat cycle</strong> before claiming fresh assistance on the same land parcel.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" aria-label="PMKSY PDMC Subsidy Rates and Funding Pattern Table" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Scheme Parameter</th>
                <th scope="col">Small &amp; Marginal Farmers (≤ 2 Hectares)</th>
                <th scope="col">Other / Large Farmers (&gt; 2 Hectares)</th>
                <th scope="col">Operational Rules &amp; Norms</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Central Base Subsidy Share</th>
                <td><strong>55%</strong> of indicative unit cost</td>
                <td><strong>45%</strong> of indicative unit cost</td>
                <td>Calculated per GoI spacing-wise unit cost matrix (GST excluded/as per state norm).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Effective Rate with State Top-Up</th>
                <td><strong>70% – 90%</strong> (varies by state)</td>
                <td><strong>55% – 80%</strong> (varies by state)</td>
                <td>Top-ups funded via State Budget or NABARD Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Centre : State Funding Split</th>
                <td>60:40 (General States) · 90:10 (NE &amp; Himalayan)</td>
                <td>60:40 (General States) · 90:10 (NE &amp; Himalayan)</td>
                <td>100% Central funding for Union Territories (UTs).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Maximum Eligible Land Area</th>
                <td>Up to 2.0 Hectares (full holding)</td>
                <td>Capped at <strong>5.0 Hectares</strong> per beneficiary</td>
                <td>Includes individual farmers, FPOs, SHGs, and registered tenant farmers.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Disbursement &amp; Repeat Cycle</th>
                <td>100% DBT post-geo-tagged verification</td>
                <td>100% DBT post-geo-tagged verification</td>
                <td>Re-application permitted on the same survey number after <strong>7 years</strong>.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="subsidy-checklist-grid">
          <article class="info-panel">
            <h3>Mandatory Documents for PDMC Application</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Aadhaar Card</strong> linked with an active mobile number and NPCI-mapped bank account.</li>
              <li><strong>Land Ownership Record:</strong> Latest RoR / 7/12 extract / Khatauni / Jamabandi (or registered lease deed where permitted by state rules).</li>
              <li><strong>Bank Passbook Copy:</strong> Showing IFSC code and account holder name for DBT settlement.</li>
              <li><strong>Caste Certificate:</strong> Required for SC/ST priority allocation and additional state top-up slabs.</li>
              <li><strong>Water Source &amp; Electricity Proof:</strong> Well/borewell/canal certificate or irrigation pump electricity bill.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="info-panel">
            <h3>How to Apply Online (Step-by-Step)</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>Register on your State Agriculture / Horticulture DBT Portal (or visit your nearest Common Service Centre).</li>
              <li>Select <strong>PMKSY – Per Drop More Crop</strong>, choose Drip or Sprinkler system, and select a BIS-certified registered manufacturer.</li>
              <li>Obtain pre-sanction / work order approval from the Block or District Agriculture Officer.</li>
              <li>Install the system, upload the GST invoice, and complete mandatory <strong>geo-tagged field inspection</strong> to receive DBT funds.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="portal-links">
              <a href="https://pmksy.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="portal-pill">PMKSY Official Portal ↗</a>
              <a href="https://pdmc.da.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="portal-pill">PDMC Dashboard ↗</a>
              <a href="https://mahadbt.maharashtra.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="portal-pill">MahaDBT Portal ↗</a>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ NATIVE HTML5 <details>/<summary> FAQ ACCORDION ============ -->
    <section class="faq" id="faq">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>
        <div class="accordion" id="faqAccordion">

          <details class="accordion-item" open>
            <summary class="accordion-summary">
              <span>What is the “Per Drop More Crop” (PDMC) scheme and how do I apply?</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="accordion-content">
              <p>PDMC is a component of PMKSY that provides financial assistance for micro-irrigation. Small/marginal farmers get <strong>55% subsidy</strong>, others get <strong>45%</strong>. To apply, visit your state’s agriculture department portal or Common Service Centre (CSC) with land records and Aadhaar. The subsidy is now transferred directly (DBT) to reduce delays.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="accordion-item">
            <summary class="accordion-summary">
              <span>Why is micro-irrigation adoption still low among small farmers if it’s so beneficial?</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="accordion-content">
              <p>While the technology is proven, the <strong>adoption gap</strong> is real. Key barriers include <strong>high upfront capital costs</strong>, <strong>delays in subsidy disbursement</strong> (reported in ~46% of cases in some regions), and a lack of technical knowledge for maintenance. The government is addressing this via the Micro Irrigation Fund and digital DBT, but awareness and last-mile credit access remain challenges.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="accordion-item">
            <summary class="accordion-summary">
              <span>What is the groundwater situation in India’s rice-wheat belt?</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="accordion-content">
              <p>It is <strong>critical</strong>. In Haryana, the stage of groundwater extraction is <strong>136.75%</strong>, with some districts exceeding 200%. In Punjab, water tables are falling by <strong>0.4–1.0 meters per year</strong>, and 111–115 of 153 assessment units are “over-exploited”. Solutions require a “landscape-scale” approach—not just farm-level efficiency, but changing cropping patterns and incentivizing low-water crops.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="accordion-item">
            <summary class="accordion-summary">
              <span>What are the most cost-effective water-saving techniques for a smallholder?</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="accordion-content">
              <p><strong>Laser land leveling</strong> (ensures uniform water distribution), <strong>mulching</strong> (reduces evaporation), and <strong>Direct Seeded Rice (DSR)</strong> are low-cost, high-impact agronomic practices. When combined with a subsidized drip system for high-value crops, the payback period can be <strong>0.5 to 2 years</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="accordion-item">
            <summary class="accordion-summary">
              <span>How does community participation help in water management?</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="accordion-content">
              <p>The <strong>Hiware Bazar model</strong> in Maharashtra is the gold standard. Through integrated watershed development and <em>Gram Sabha</em> participation, the village raised its water table from 20–25 feet to 70–80 feet, changed cropping patterns to high-value horticulture, and saw <strong>reverse migration</strong>. It proves that top-down policy must be paired with local ownership for long-term sustainability.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

